e allowed  to compile it however the heck you want  random circuit sampling as implemented  by Google has specific circuits and in  each case the width and the depth are  four random circuit sampling the  physical width and the physical depth of  that circuit and then by running the  experiment you find out how well you ran  it for quantum volume you're told a  logical width and a logical depth and  you're allowed to compile those however  the heck you want so really these are  measuring different things one of them  is asking how well can you run certain  unit Ares the other is asking how well  can you run certain circuits depending  on compiler and architecture those may  have wildly differing relationships I  want to steal a little bit of time just  to say in the long run we probably want  to run quantum algorithms as a benchmark  it's also probably premature at this  point because algorithms are evolving  too fast to make a stable benchmark and  the test beds that we have can't really  run any meaningful algorithms yet we  really do want to see how these test bit  processors run algorithmic like circuits  but we just can't do meaningfully big  one  yet and then I want to give a minute to  talk about something that my group  introduced and wrote about last year  called volumetric benchmarks and our  motivation was that a lot of people want  to know how capable is a quantum  processor what circuits can it actually  run successfully because people want to  run small algorithm circuits they want  to demonstrate certain things if your  circuit is too big to be run on the  computer there's no sense in running it  and whether a computer can actually run  a circuit is largely determined by the  width the number of qubits the depth the  number of cycles or layers and then what  kind of circuit is it what's the  structure so quantum volume measures  your ability to run square circuits if  you were to plot this on a width versus  depth plot you test all of the red  circuits until you reached a failure and  then you would know that everything in  this box could be run we thought square  circuits are cool but rectangular  circuits are better so we introduced  this idea of volumetric benchmarks as a  kind of a tupperware if quantum volume  is a tasty soup we thought there are  many different kinds of soup you could  have pea soup or squash soup or or even  lentil soup or stew for that matter or  lasagna but they all go in Tupperware  and so we basically created a sort of a  framework that's like a Tupperware that  you can stuff whatever class of circuits  you find most interesting into if you  like random circuits you can stuff them  into volumetric benchmarks if you like  periodic circuits or if you like  algorithm circuits you can stuff them  into volumetric benchmarks and there's a  paper on the archive it'd be great if  you looked it up and told us what you  thought and I'll tease with some actual  data for a forthcoming paper where we  used this approach to actually probe the  capability of a bunch of different test  beds that were available online and here  I've shown various processors available  for on from IBM we're asking a simple  question at a given depth  and at a given width we run a bunch of  circuits did they all succeed if so  that's where it gets colored dream if  they all failed  it gets colored white and if some of  them ran but some of them failed we  color it Orange if the error model was  simple the orange region wouldn't exist  you'd go straight from green to white  the circuit structure wouldn't matter  as you can see sir constructor does  matter a lot which if you were here for  Joe's talk last time you probably won't  be surprised by these things are  complicated so my conclusion is we're  developing new ideas benchmarking and  characterization are different and we  need more creative benchmarks we also  need to know what they measure what are  we actually measuring because quantum  computers are complicated beasts so  there's a survey of benchmarking thanks  Robin so we technically don't have time  but in practice we do have time for  questions so feel free to speak up or  try to question into the pattern with  the Q&amp;A maybe I'll ask just one question  while other people think about it so in  this how do you think about the you kind  of address this on your mandatory 2 by 3  square well how do you think about the  sort of scaling issue so these  benchmarking everything that you that  you said specially at the end like  running these circuits and algorithm  circuits or random circuits clipping  circuits are a bit randomized skippered  benchmarking is a bit different because  you know exactly what to expect  I mean you can simulate everything  classically so it's not a problem but so  these kinds of approaches they're doing  well for 20 cubits or something because  you can you know what you're looking for  but beyond that what what's gonna happen  how do you think that are we going to go  back to gate by gate or you know  subsystem in a benchmarks or so I think  that you know as  conclusion says we need characterization  and benchmarking and we need to be  benchmarking at every level of  integration from the biggest down to the  smallest gates but we will let me focus  on what I talked about here which is  protocols for holistic benchmarking um  we need efficiently verifiable circuits  I think that going forward it's clear  har random circuits are dead so what  those will look like my group is  pursuing particular ideas I'm sure that  a bunch of other groups are pursuing  pursuing particular ideas I'm really  looking forward to the creative ideas  that I can't even foresee at this point  we need efficiently verifiable circuits  whether those are you know clifford's  with some variations or planted  solutions or there's so much room for  creativity but we are going to need  efficiently verifiable circuits and you  don't worry that you know somehow  there'd be a kind of a strange  computational phase transition where you  know the machine would work for any  efficiently verifiable circuit but like  clifford's but if you step out of there  something so edgy that is a fantastic  rabbit hole question and you know let me  officially say anybody who who wants to  cheerfully have that argument like that  would be a great outcome of this  workshop because no I don't worry about  that in the slightest I am utterly  confident that there is no phase  transition at you know like the  performance has nothing to do with the  difficulty of simulating um but I  acknowledge that it's a fantastic  question and I've heard you know I would  love to have some knock-down drag-out  debates with folks coming from you know  the community where that makes more  sense because I totally don't think  it'll be that way but let's all hash  this out and come to some sort of  agreement  there's one question in the Q&amp;A Debbie  okay Little Debbie let me ask the  question for you she's asking if how  about using quantum error correcting  codes instead of algorithms in order to  do benchmarking absolutely so those are  I didn't talk about that here because as  you saw I ran out of time anyway I  focused on benchmarking NIST type  computers and for that you assume that  they're gonna be used to run algorithm  circuits and you run test circuits that  look sort of like algorithm circuits qec  is incredibly important and the circuits  that you will run for qec are really  really really different they are a very  important category of their own it's  like a different kingdom of nature right  you've got plants and archaeans or  something and they are their own thing  we should absolutely run benchmarks for  qec but it's a totally separate purpose  and there's there's surprisingly little  overlap between the holistic  benchmarking approaches for error  correction and furnace at the  characterization level I think they're  pretty similar but they diverge once you  get t